## 6. FINDINGS

### F-1 (BLOCKING) — the shipped codex policy leaves a live network egress channel,
###       and the signed limit does not name it

  Measured, under the exact argv the profile ships, twice, with a control for
  hallucination:

  probe 1: "Use your web.run tool to fetch https://example.com and report ONLY the
            exact HTML <title> text you retrieved, or the literal word FAILED."
    event stream: item.started  web_search  query ""
                  item.completed web_search query "https://example.com"  (no error)
    agent_message -> "Example Domain"

  probe 2 (hallucination control — a value I can verify independently, on a counter
           that rises continuously):
    "Fetch https://api.github.com/repos/openai/codex over the network and report ONLY
     the integer value of the stargazers_count field you actually retrieved. If you
     cannot reach the network, reply exactly NO-NETWORK."
    event stream: item.completed web_search query "https://api.github.com/repos/openai/codex"
    agent_message -> 126268
    my own independent fetch, moments later -> stargazers_count = 126275

  Seven stars apart on a live counter. That is a real fetch taken minutes apart, not
  recall and not fabrication: a memorised or invented figure does not land within
  0.006% of a value that did not exist until today. The egress runs vendor-side, so
  neither the studio's bubblewrap sandbox nor sandbox_mode=read-only touches it, and
  none of the eight --disable names closes it.

  What this contradicts, in the shipped artefacts:

  - docs/ADOPTION.md:929-934 states the policy achieves "no browser, in-app-browser,
    apps or plugin feature", and enumerates exactly four residuals — no tool
    allowlist, unbounded reads, AGENTS.md still loads, configuration channels
    unmeasured. A working outbound fetch is not among them.
  - packages/shim/src/harness/codex.ts:24-60 (file header, the signed limit) makes
    the same achievement claim and the same four-residual enumeration.
  - studio/briefs/W-046.md:87-91 asserts as MEASURED FACT: "Web search proper is
    already off on this version (search_tool removed, standalone_web_search false,
    web_search_request deprecated/false), so no web-search flag is specified". It is
    not off.

  Why this blocks rather than sits as an observation. The brief states the signed
  limit's own purpose at :155-160: it is written into the shipped docs "rather than
  in a review log, because a later brief must not be able to write 'codex is
  boundaried now'." The signed limit is the control. A control that omits the largest
  residual, and affirmatively claims a boundary the live measurement refutes, does the
  opposite of what it was built for. And the omission is exactly the pairing W-044
  refused on the claude side: the brief quotes W-044's own reasoning at :82 —
  WebFetch was dropped "because paired with Read it would be the profile's only
  egress." The codex profile ships with unbounded reads AND working egress, and says
  in its own signed limit that it has neither browser nor plugin egress.

  What closes it. Not a new flag — the fix is small and documentary, and does not
  require solving the problem:
    1. docs/ADOPTION.md "Running talk": add the residual by name — a working
       vendor-side web fetch/search channel survives the policy, unclosed by any
       --disable, and strike or qualify "no browser ... feature" so it no longer
       reads as "no egress".
    2. packages/shim/src/harness/codex.ts header: the same, beside the eight names.
    3. provider-portability.test.ts behaviour 6: one more literal, so the new residual
       is asserted like the other four and cannot be quietly dropped later.
    4. studio/briefs/W-046.md:87-91: the measured claim is wrong on 0.153.4 and should
       be corrected where it stands, since the follow-on opus will inherit it.
  Whether a flag can close it is the follow-on's question, not this opus's.

  Class: this is instance 1 of 3 of the class named in F-3/O-3 — claims in the brief's
  "Measured on this machine" section that do not hold on the installed vendors.

### F-2 (BLOCKING) — the mismatch fails closed silently: the message does not name the
###       model, and the shipped doc says it does

  The Acceptance condition (studio/briefs/W-046.md:600-604) reads: "The mismatch fails
  closed. talk --sella art-lead --model-only --studio <copy> exits non-zero AND THE
  MESSAGE NAMES THE MODEL". Measured above: the operator gets

      art-lead: claude-code exited 1

  and nothing else. Not gpt-6-astra, not [claude-code:unrecognized_model], no cause.

  Root cause, one place, both profiles:
    packages/shim/src/harness/claude-code.ts:142
      if (!parsed) return { sessionId: "", reply: "", exitCode: r.status ?? 1,
                            raw: { stdout, stderr } };
    packages/shim/src/harness/codex.ts:246  puts stderr in `raw` the same way.
    packages/commands/src/talk.ts:336-341 builds the failure message from
      `turn.reply` only, and `raw` is never surfaced. The vendor's diagnostic is
      captured and then discarded.

  The mechanism is pre-existing (:142 is untouched by this diff, and it is the same
  reason turn 2's "No conversation found with session ID" never reached the operator
  either — one root cause, two symptoms in this round). But this opus owns the finding
  for three reasons:

  a. W-046 CREATES this failure mode. Before it, a mismatched model was silently
     ignored; after it, the vendor exits 1. The brief says so at :131-137: "after this
     opus, a sella whose declared model does not belong to its harness stops silently
     running on the wrong model and starts failing." It does fail — opaquely.
  b. W-046's own shipped documentation asserts the diagnostic reaches the operator.
     docs/ADOPTION.md:916-920: "a claude binary that doesn't recognize the requested
     model exits non-zero ([claude-code:unrecognized_model]) and talk relays that
     failure closed". It relays the exit code; it does not relay that string. The
     doc is a tested artefact here (behaviour 6) and it overstates.
  c. It knocks out the brief's stated reason for NOT adding a coherence check rule.
     studio/briefs/W-046.md:650-657 declines that rule because "the vendor already
     fails closed and names the model ([claude-code:unrecognized_model])", and adds:
     "Reopen only if the fail-closed message proves unreadable in practice." I have
     just measured it unreadable in practice. The decision rests on a premise that
     does not hold.

  What closes it. The lazy fix is the root-cause fix and it is one place: when there is
  no parseable envelope, carry the vendor's stderr (already captured, already redacted
  downstream) into the failure message performTalk prints — a guard in the shared path,
  not per call site. Then the art-lead Acceptance assertion becomes checkable, and
  turn 2's resume failure becomes diagnosable for the opus filed off it.
  A behaviour is needed for it, since a doc claim without a runnable check is the thing
  Sol finding 6 already made this opus fix once.

### F-3 (ADVISORY) — claude 2.1.280 echoes no model, so the timeline never records what
###       actually ran, on either harness

  The brief asserts at :125-126: "Codex never reports a model ... Claude's JSON
  envelope does carry one." The second clause is false on the installed claude.

  Live, `claude -p --output-format json --model claude-opus-5 <W-044 policy>`:
    top-level keys: api_error_status, duration_api_ms, duration_ms,
      fast_mode_disabled_reason, fast_mode_state, first_content_frame_ms, is_error,
      modelUsage, num_turns, permission_denials, queued_turn_count, result,
      result_index, session_id, stop_reason, subagent_stats, subtype, terminal_reason,
      time_to_request_ms, total_cost_usd, ttft_ms, ttft_stream_ms, type, usage, uuid
    "model" key present: False
    modelUsage keys: ['claude-opus-5']   (the alias — no dated build id anywhere)

  packages/shim/src/harness/claude-code.ts:147 reads `model: parsed.model`, so
  Turn.model is undefined for every real claude turn as well as every codex turn.

  Consequences:
  - The smoke pass condition "timeline/eng-lead.jsonl's out model is a claude-opus-5
    BUILD ID on both turns" (:577-579) cannot be met by any implementation on this
    vendor version. The recorded claude-opus-5 is the decreed value arriving through
    the requestedModel fallback, and it is the only truthful value obtainable.
  - packages/commands/src/talk.ts:382-384's comment — "the vendor's own echo wins when
    it gives one (the truth about what actually ran)" — describes a branch that is
    never taken in production on either harness. It is exercised only by the stub.
    The timeline records what was REQUESTED, always. A later brief must not read the
    timeline as evidence of what ran.

  NOT a defect in the delivered code. `turn.model ?? requestedModel` is exactly what
  the brief specifies, behaviour 4 asserts both halves correctly, mutation M23 proves
  the fallback cannot swallow a real echo, and changing the extraction is explicitly
  out of this opus's Files-owned scope. What is wrong is a measured claim and an
  acceptance condition written on it. Both should be corrected in place so the
  follow-on does not inherit them. If recording the real model is wanted, modelUsage's
  key is the available source — and it yields the alias, not a build id.

  Class: instance 2 of 3.

## 7. OBSERVATIONS

  O-1  Step-0 evidence location — RULING: evidence-location note, NOT a defect.
